{"id":"generic-titles","title":"Titles that say nothing about the work","tier":"REDFLAG","question":"How many people hold titles such as Manager II, Special Assistant 14 or Project Manager 3, what do they cost, and where did the numbers grow?","finding":"People paid in generic-title classes rose from 1,829 in fiscal 2013 to 3,555 in fiscal 2026, and their share of all City pay from 6.7% to 10.7% ($604.0 million in fiscal 2026). The Manager grades grew from 812 to 1,361 people (+68%) and the Administrative Analyst and Management Assistant grades from 758 to 1,760 (+132%), while the number of people paid by the City overall grew by 8%. The largest added cost is in Public Health (Manager I to VIII: 106 to 213 people, $30.4 million more a year).","how":"City payroll file, fiscal 2013 to 2026. A generic title is one made of a general word and a grade: Manager I to VIII (including the transportation agency’s own Manager classes), Deputy Director and Department Head I to V, numbered Special Assistant and Mayoral Staff classes, Project Manager 1 to 4, and the Administrative Analyst and Management Assistant grades. People are counted once per group and year if paid in it at all; pay is all pay in the class. Each row compares one department’s first and latest year; the share is of that department’s whole pay bill. Board seats, elected offices and the Superior Court are left out. Ranked by added yearly cost.","limits":"Pay rose for every class over these years, so cost grows even where numbers do not; the share-of-pay columns allow for that. A generic title is how the City’s pay plan names its management and analyst grades, and the working title on the job announcement is usually specific. Growth can reflect new programmes, work moved in-house from contractors, or older specific classes being folded into the general ones. The payroll cannot say what any of these people do; the position description can.","columns":[{"k":"department","t":"Department","type":"text"},{"k":"kind","